course: dictionary
lesson: 10708
title: Untie: undoing knots
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word untie is made of un- and tie. The prefix un- often means "undo", so to untie something is to undo a tie: to undo a knot or a bow, or to take off a rope or string that is holding something.
explain: Think of your shoelaces. When you untie them, the knot comes undone and the laces come loose. «He unties the tow-rope, jumps on the horse and gallops off» shows the rope being undone, so the horse is free to go.
explain: Look at what happens after the knot or string is undone: the thing it was holding is set free. «Air rushing out of an untied balloon» makes sense because once the neck of the balloon is no longer tied shut, the air can escape.
fact: Untie is a verb. Its forms are untie, unties, untied and untying.
fact: To untie means to undo a knot or a bow, or to take off a rope or string that is holding something.
fact: Untie is the opposite of tie: you tie a knot, and later you untie it.
fact: You untie a ribbon on a present, your shoelaces, or a boat from the dock.
fact: «An untied balloon» means a balloon whose neck is no longer tied shut, so air can escape.
exercise: «He unties the tow-rope, jumps on the horse and gallops off.» What does unties mean here? | Ties tightly again | Pulls the horse closer | Hides | *Undoes the knot on
exercise: Ella wants to open her present. What must she do to the ribbon first? | Wrap it tighter | Tie it into a bigger bow | Put it on her head | *Untie it
exercise: «Air rushing out of an untied balloon» suggests the balloon is... | Tied in a tight knot | Full of water | Painted red | *Not tied shut, so air can escape
test: «The boat was held by a rope, and he untied it and rowed away.» What does untied mean here? | Lost the rope | *Undid the rope that held it | Tied it up again | Threw the rope into the sea
test: Which phrase means the opposite of tie a knot? | *Untie the knot | Knot the rope twice | Make a bigger knot | Wind up the string
exam: Jayden says untie is the opposite of tie. Which sentence uses untie correctly? | The hero ties the prisoner's ropes tighter | I untie a sandwich into crumbs | I untie the sky when it rains | *I untie my shoelaces to take my trainers off
